Found during k74 (worker). `available` and `install` resolve url sources (now under the sync lock) and may replace the cache, but the version (etag/last-modified) is only recorded in the lock by a sync. The next sync then sends the older lock etag in If-None-Match; offline, the newer files are labelled with the old etag; if the server goes back to that etag, a 304 keeps the newer files under the old label. Fix idea: store the version next to the cache dir and compare/send that. Test in test/url-cache.test.ts.

2026-09-26 16:27 Design: the url cache owns its version. publishEntries writes a reserved root file '.skilletor-version' (the SourceLocation version string) into the stage tree before the swap, so tree and label travel together through stage/backup/restore and the k74 sweep. UrlSource.resolve reads it for If-None-Match (etag: only), for the 304 answer and as the offline/failure label; the caller's version is no longer consulted. A cache without the file (written by <=0.3.0, or a corrupt file) is unknown: unconditional GET, offline label 'unknown'. check() keeps comparing with what the last sync read (k80) – an available/install refresh must still read as changed so a sync installs the newer tree. With no backend using it, the resolve(cachedVersion) parameter goes from the Source interface; the k83 resolver key drops the url version (every name of one URL gets the one resolve; the 304 answers the cache's own ETag). Also fixes k80's note (sync sent the first lock entry's version). Catalog never scans source-root files, so the file is invisible to items; archive entries of that name are dropped.

2026-09-26 16:28 Tests first: 5 new k82 tests in test/url-cache.test.ts (available refresh → next sync sends/records the cache's ETag "2"; server back at the lock's "1" serves its tree, no 304 for the newer one; offline after refresh → label "2"; cache without version file → unconditional GET then conditional, offline label 'unknown'; archive entry named like the version file is dropped). Existing url-cache tests drop the version argument (they now assert the 304/fallback label comes from the cache). k83 url test in shared-resolve rewritten: one request for all names of one URL, conditional on the cache's ETag. Red before the fix: 13 failing, each for the claimed reason.

